A||||4||MooCow||My radiator is leaking!!||||9||
Z||000000||MooCow||09-05-2000||11:28 PM||||Well my radiator has sprung a leak someplace cause antifreeze is leaking really badley so I guess I am just gonna put it up for teh winter.  Any suggestion before putting it up what I should do to "winterize" it???<BR>I know that I am going to get a full tank of gas so I can start it every week like I did for my truck and also going to buy a thing of antifreeze since it leaks now so I dont over heat the engine.  Also am putting a tarp over it so the snow will not sit on the car but will slide off.  Anything else I should do???  The engine I really don't care if it never start again cause next year is the new engine time so it only had to make it throught the summer and it almsot made.<P>                            Thanks Ken||||reg||9
Z||000001||GT Bastard||09-06-2000||08:09 AM||||C'mon man, it's not that hard to remove the radiator and have it fixed.  Just have them patch it if you can't afford a new one.  Nobody can go ALL winter without their Fiero.||||reg||10
Z||000002||whamel||09-06-2000||08:23 AM||||Yeah, I had mine out in under and hour once I got over fooling with that stupid coolant overflow bottle.  It's a pretty easy process and I did a rough outline of the steps I went through on my web page if you're interested.  <BR>With the winters we tend to have, you want to make sure that radiator is in good shape unless you're keeping the car inside!||||reg||1
Z||000003||whamel||09-06-2000||08:25 AM||||Oh, the downside is..if you have a V-6 I couldn't find a single used radiator in our area that wasn't already popped or trashed and most of the auto parts stores want between $130 and $180 for a new one.  Best to get it checked out and patched if you can.<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||09-06-2000||12:02 PM||||MooCow,  Putting it up for the winter may not be the worst thing if you are planning on a new engine in the spring.  When I first bought my 86SE it had a small leak at one of the water pump hoses.  I fixed that, then the radiator blew.  Replaced that and the heater core went.  I finally figured out that 14 year old metal and rubber was not going to hold pressure so I replaced the heater core and every heater/coolant hose in the entire car.  If you fix the radiator now you may be fixing leaks all winter, or just plan on replacing everything when the car gets a new engine.||||reg||1
